MediaCodec: choose a video encoder that exists, and replace one that will not encode

and_vid_mediacodec.cpp picks its MediaCodec component from four hardcoded
lists of OMX component names, preferring the software one. Android 12 retired
those names and Android 15 ships none of them, but
AMediaCodec_createCodecByName("OMX.google.h264.encoder") still returns a
handle, so codec_exists() accepts a component the platform cannot honour. Every
dequeueInputBuffer on it then fails for the life of the call: the camera
captures, the preview draws, and the RTP stream carries 0 packets/s while the
far end holds its first frame.

Measured handset-to-handset on 2026-09-23. A Galaxy M14 (SM-M146B, Android 15)
advertises only c2.android.avc.encoder and c2.exynos.h264.encoder and sent no
video at all; a Galaxy M23 (SM-E236B, Android 14), which still has the OMX
names, was unaffected — which is what made it look like a network fault.

This patch:

  * enumerates every encoder the platform advertises for the MIME, through
    Java's MediaCodecList (the NDK has no enumeration API before API 36),
    instead of guessing at names;

  * ranks them hardware-Codec2 first, then any other hardware, then Codec2
    software, then the rest, using MediaCodecInfo.isHardwareAccelerated() where
    it exists (API 29+) and a name heuristic below that;

  * keeps the static lists as the fallback for a device whose codecs cannot be
    enumerated, so nothing that worked before can regress;

  * condemns an encoder that fails in a real call — one that will not configure,
    or one that configures and then starves for AND_MEDIA_STARVED_LIMIT
    consecutive frames — and re-chooses. A configure failure is repaired inside
    the same codec open, so the call that hits it still gets video.

The probe at start-up does no more than create the component and delete it.
Configuring and starting a candidate there — the only way to learn more about it
without a call — never returned on an SM-E236B when tearing down an OMX.qcom
encoder whose configure had just failed, leaving discovery stuck and the account
unregistered. A call that picks a poor encoder is a bad call; a stack that
cannot register is a dead phone, so the stronger check lives in the codec open
where a failure unwinds safely.

